    "created_at": "2019-04-13T18:55:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-04-13T18:55:39Z",
    "body": "Nice initiative @matchai, not sure that I'll have time to contribute right now, but I will be following this project very closely 👍 🎉 \r\n\r\nMy suggestion would be to first focus on parallel execution and expanding the proof-of-concept with demonstrating asynchronous rendering, because this can significantly impact your future architecture. I haven't actually tried this project, but from what I understand by quickly looking at the sources, you execute `starship $status` command, wait for it to compute everything, and then draw prompt based on the result.\r\n\r\nConsider creating two sections, `slow_1` (which sleeps 5 seconds and returns string \"slow_1\") and `slow_2` (which sleeps 10 seconds and returns string \"slow_2\"). The possible behaviors:\r\n\r\n- Sequential execution, prompt will get stuck for 15 seconds evaluating everything, then renders everything at once - naive implementation\r\n- Fake parallel execution, where prompt gets stuck only for 10 seconds running `starship` command which evaluates both sections in parallel, then after 10 seconds renders everything - I believe this is what you have right now\r\n- True parallel execution, on zsh with the use of [zsh-async](https://github.com/mafredri/zsh-async), where prompt is not stuck at any moment of time, you can always type, but after 5 and 10 seconds since startup prompt is re-rendered - this is what I currently have [in my fork of spaceship](https://github.com/maximbaz/spaceship-prompt).\r\n\r\nHopefully this won't require a lot of zsh code and you will still be able to keep most of the logic written in Rust, and hopefully something analogous to `zsh-async` exists for Fish 🙂 ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
