    "created_at": "2019-04-13T19:30:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-04-13T19:58:13Z",
    "body": "Good call @maximbaz!\r\n\r\nI was definitely thinking about how parallelization could be implemented. \r\n\r\n> Fake parallel execution ... I believe this is what you have right now\r\n\r\nAt the moment, the overhead of parallelization caused the prompt to run slower than if it were to run entirely sequentially, but that is definitely going to be added as more segments are implemented.\r\n\r\n> True parallel execution\r\n\r\nzsh-async is that it relies on `WINCH` kill signals to update the prompt, which other async fish prompts also do. It could certainly be worthwhile to compile two binary outputs: standard and parallel via `WINCH`. \r\n\r\nI'd be hesitant to create only the latter is because it's reliant on a hacky implementation that I think future shells may not use (e.g. Fish plans to implement its own parallelization feature). It also would be incompatible with other existing shells: bash, elvish, etc.\r\n\r\nI'll see if I can get that working this week. 😄 ",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
