    "created_at": "2019-05-02T16:31:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-02T16:31:50Z",
    "body": "Hard to recall all of them. Here are some:\r\n\r\n1. All of the issues regarding async rendering.\r\n2. Startup issues. Right now you just execute the program on every prompt displaying. In the case of Node.js, this takes a significant amount of time.\r\nSpaceship ZSH loads slowly, but only once (when you open your terminal for the first time). After that, all functions, utils, etc are stored in the runtime, so drawing the prompt is relatively fast.\r\nI have high hopes for C or Rust as the system languages. Hopefully, they will execute the prompt faster. But still, you have to do it for every displaying. \r\n3. Similar issue with executing commands-helpers, i.e when you need to get the `node` version you have to execute `node -v`. Even when the prompt itself will be fast, you still have to wait for those commands to finish. I think some kind of caching mechanism could solve this issue.\r\n4. Runtime variables access. Let's take a look at [`docker` section](https://github.com/denysdovhan/spaceship-prompt/blob/master/sections/docker.zsh), for example. To render it properly we have to check some variables, like `COMPOSE_FILE`, `COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR`, `DOCKER_MACHINE_NAME`, some of them are not _environment_ variables meaning you don't have access to them from the separate process. You can access them only within zsh/bash/fish/etc runtime.\r\n\r\nThere were other issues as well, but I'm pretty sure they're solvable. Nevertheless, consider issues from above seriously. They might be tricky.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
